{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":5000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Otis"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Otis:\nThis article is about a Category 5 Pacific hurricane in 2023. For other storms of the same name, see [List of storms named Otis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_storms_named_Otis \"List of storms named Otis\").\n\nHurricane Otis\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| Hurricane Otis approaching [Guerrero](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guerrero \"Guerrero\") at peak intensity on October 25 | |\n| Meteorological history | |\n| Formed | October 22, 2023 |\n| Dissipated | October 25, 2023 |\n| Category 5 major hurricane | |\n| *1-minute sustained* ([SSHWS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saffir%E2%80%93Simpson_scale \"Saffir–Simpson scale\")/[NWS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Weather_Service \"National Weather Service\")) | |\n| Highest winds | 165 mph (270 km/h) |\n| Lowest pressure | 922 [mbar](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bar_(unit) \"Bar (unit)\") ([hPa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pascal_(unit) \"Pascal (unit)\")); 27.23 [inHg](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inch_of_mercury \"Inch of mercury\") |\n| Overall effects | |\n| Fatalities | ≥52 total[[nb 1]](#cite_note-disputed-2) |\n| Missing | ≥32[[nb 1]](#cite_note-disputed-2) |\n| Damage | $12–16 billion (2023 [USD](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USD \"USD\")) (Costliest Pacific hurricane on record; costliest in Mexican history) |\n| Areas affected | Southern [Mexico](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico \"Mexico\") |\n| [IBTrACS](https://ncics.org/ibtracs/index.php?name=v04r01-2023294N09264)[Edit this at Wikidata](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q123178445#P4540 \"Edit this at Wikidata\") / [[2]](#cite_note-TCR-3) | |\n| ---  Part of the **[2023 Pacific hurricane season](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Pacific_hurricane_season \"2023 Pacific hurricane season\")** | |\n\n**Hurricane Otis** was a compact but very powerful [tropical cyclone](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_cyclone \"Tropical cyclone\") that made a devastating [landfall](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landfall \"Landfall\") near the Mexican city of [Acapulco](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acapulco \"Acapulco\") as a [Category 5 hurricane](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Category_5_Pacific_hurricanes \"List of Category 5 Pacific hurricanes\") in October 2023. Otis was the first [Pacific hurricane](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_hurricane \"Pacific hurricane\") on record to make landfall at Category 5 intensity and became the strongest Pacific hurricane to make landfall, surpassing [Hurricane Patricia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Patricia \"Hurricane Patricia\") of 2015. The resulting damage made Otis the costliest tropical cyclone to strike Mexico on record, surpassing [Hurricane Wilma](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Wilma \"Hurricane Wilma\") of 2005.[[2]](#cite_note-TCR-3) The fifteenth tropical storm, tenth hurricane, eighth major hurricane,[[nb 2]](#cite_note-5) and second Category 5 hurricane of the [2023 Pacific hurricane season](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Pacific_hurricane_season \"2023 Pacific hurricane season\"), Otis originated from a disturbance several hundred miles south of the [Gulf of Tehuantepec](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf_of_Tehuantepec \"Gulf of Tehuantepec\"). Initially forecast to stay offshore and to only be a weak tropical storm at peak intensity, Otis instead underwent [explosive intensification](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Explosive_intensification \"Explosive intensification\") to reach peak winds of 165 mph (270 km/h) and weakened only slightly before making landfall as a powerful Category 5 hurricane. Once inland, the hurricane quickly weakened before dissipating the following day.\n\nMaking landfall just west of Acapulco, Otis's powerful winds severely damaged many of the buildings in the city. Landslides and flooding resulted from heavy rain. Communication was heavily cut off, initially leaving information about the hurricane's impact largely unknown. In the aftermath, the city had no drinking water and many residents also lost power. The government of [Guerrero](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guerrero \"Guerrero\") mobilized thousands of military members to aid survivors and assist in recovery efforts. Thousands of recovery items were sent out to those affected and donations were sent out to each of the affected families.\n\nThe hurricane caused at least 52 deaths and left 32 others missing. Total damage from Otis was estimated to be in the billions of dollars (2023 [USD](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_dollar \"United States dollar\")), with several agencies estimating $12–16 billion in damage, making it the costliest Pacific hurricane on record, surpassing [Hurricane Manuel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Manuel \"Hurricane Manuel\") in 2013.\n\n## Meteorological history\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Hurricane_Otis&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: Meteorological history\")]\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Otis_2023_path.png/330px-Otis_2023_path.png)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Otis_2023_path.png)\n\nMap plotting the storm's \n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 5000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
