{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":5000,"url":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/nathanielparishflannery/2023/11/02/the-aftermath-of-hurricane-otis-highlights-broader-problems-in-mexico/"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://www.forbes.com/sites/nathanielparishflannery/2023/11/02/the-aftermath-of-hurricane-otis-highlights-broader-problems-in-mexico/:\n![TOPSHOT-MEXICO-WEATHER-HURRICANE-OTIS-LOPEZ OBRADOR](https://imageio.forbes.com/specials-images/imageserve/654264724757a9b5309d9eb4/TOPSHOT-MEXICO-WEATHER-HURRICANE-OTIS-LOPEZ-OBRADOR/0x0.jpg?width=960)\n\nTOPSHOT - Mexican President Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador looks out of the window as the vehicle transporting his is stuck in mud during a visit to the Kilometro 42 community, near Acapulco, Guerrero State, Mexico, after the passage of Hurricane Otis, on October 25, 2023. Mexican authorities rushed to send emergency aid, restore communications and assess damage in the Pacific beach resort of Acapulco on Wednesday after a powerful hurricane left a trail of destruction and tourists stranded. (Photo by RODRIGO OROPEZA / AFP) (Photo by RODRIGO OROPEZA/AFP via Getty Images)\n\nAFP via Getty Images\n\nThroughout his time in office, Mexico’s current president, Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador, [has embraced photo opportunities with soldiers](https://jacobin.com/2022/09/amlo-mexico-ukraine-peace-military-internal-security \"https://jacobin.com/2022/09/amlo-mexico-ukraine-peace-military-internal-security\"), and used his role as commander-in-chief of the armed forces to project an image of strength and consolidated state capacity. Recently when Mexico was caught by surprise by a category 5 hurricane that hit the coastal city of Acapulco, which is home to nearly one million residents, Lopez Obrador sent in soldiers as he scrambled to coordinate a response for a disaster that inflicted damage that will cost up to [$15 billion](https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/mexico-announces-plan-rebuild-acapulco-after-hurricane-2023-11-01/ \"https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/mexico-announces-plan-rebuild-acapulco-after-hurricane-2023-11-01/\") to repair. The image of Mexico’s president traveling to survey storm damage in Acapulco, sitting inside a military Jeep, [stuck in the mud](https://www.animalpolitico.com/politica/amlo-varado-carretera-acapulco-huracan-otis \"https://www.animalpolitico.com/politica/amlo-varado-carretera-acapulco-huracan-otis\"), wheels spinning, while soldiers struggle to push him forward, is an apt visual metaphor for the broader problems with Lopez Obrador’s governing strategy.\n\nBut, more generally in Mexico, when it comes to the role of the military in society, it’s not [Acapulco](https://www.theguardian.com/cities/2016/dec/13/gunfire-tourist-resort-acapulco-mexico-torn-apart-violence \"https://www.theguardian.com/cities/2016/dec/13/gunfire-tourist-resort-acapulco-mexico-torn-apart-violence\") but rather a nearby town in the state of Guerrero called [Ayotzinapa](https://apnews.com/article/ayotzinapa-obrador-march-dissapearing-protest-guerrero-411f9f467a22080095c0ae301586904c \"https://apnews.com/article/ayotzinapa-obrador-march-dissapearing-protest-guerrero-411f9f467a22080095c0ae301586904c\") that is most frequently at the center of the discussion.\n\nIn the fall of 2014, the fallout after disappearance of 43 students from the Ayotzinapa teachers’ college in the south-western state of Guerrero quickly gathered steam, and reporters from around the globe tuned in to follow the unfolding investigation. Lopez Obrador was an aspiring presidential candidate in 2014 and he quickly seized on the Ayotzinapa incident as a rallying cry to help organize protests to turn public opinion against the centrist administration of then president Enrique Peña Nieto.\n\nThe Ayotzinapa incident fueled speculation about corrupt links between drug trafficking groups, government officials, and senior, active duty members of the military.\n\nThe rallying cry at a series of protests organized in Guerrero and in other parts of Mexico was “[*fue el estado*](https://www.nytimes.com/2022/08/18/world/americas/mexico-students-disappearance.html \"https://www.nytimes.com/2022/08/18/world/americas/mexico-students-disappearance.html\")!” it was the state.\n\nAt the time, Lopez Obrador demanded justice, and criticized the rampant impunity that has long protected the politically powerful in Mexico.\n\nThe Ayotzinapa incident helped harness popular frustration with the status quo in Mexican politics, and helped catapult Lopez Obrador to a landslide victory in the 2018 election.\n\nMORE FROM\n\nFORBES ADVISOR\n\n[![Graphic Best High-Yield Savings Accounts Of September 2023](https://thumbor.forbes.com/thumbor/fit-in/1290x/https://www.forbes.com/advisor/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/getty_1-best-online-savings-thumbnail_101920pm.jpg)](https://www.forbes.com/advisor/banking/savings/best-high-yield-savings-accounts/?utm_source=forbes&utm_medium=recirc&utm_campaign=tiger-sept23)\n\n[![Graphic Best 5% Interest Savings Accounts of September 2023](https://thumbor.forbes.com/thumbor/fit-in/900x510/https://www.forbes.com/advisor/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Saving-Rates-2.jpg)](https://www.forbes.com/advisor/banking/savings/best-5-percent-interest-savings-accounts/?utm_source=forbes&utm_medium=recirc&utm_campaign=tiger-sept23)\n\nAs president, however, he has ignored longstanding calls from \n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 5000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
