{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":5500,"start_index":5000,"url":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/nathanielparishflannery/2023/11/02/the-aftermath-of-hurricane-otis-highlights-broader-problems-in-mexico/"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://www.forbes.com/sites/nathanielparishflannery/2023/11/02/the-aftermath-of-hurricane-otis-highlights-broader-problems-in-mexico/:\nlegal scholars, public policy experts, security analysts, and human rights experts who have for years advocated that Mexico should stop relying so heavily [on the military to patrol streets](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qvDQqS17dFY \"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qvDQqS17dFY\") and ambush criminal gangs.\n\nLopez Obrador once promised that if elected he would order the army to return to the barracks. As president, however, he has [embraced Mexico’s armed forces](https://www.googleadservices.com/pagead/aclk?sa=L&ai=DChcSEwixqIf1hqOCAxWcAa0GHShoCYAYABACGgJwdg&gclid=CjwKCAjw7oeqBhBwEiwALyHLM1DNptS0iB-D6AtfkTWq-VQATKNUDrIC1e9c-uQZ9ihYnIFkkZRo1RoCzrMQAvD_BwE&ohost=www.google.com&cid=CAESV-D2E7mvwwka2aX5l98QaBPJPDPKssYk8phlIsqH5pC3RKvnIZAiXOT2GAhDC2jOE0-BlL1sXwLIpPoQxg6bOrmeIeH9ECbTc0jTNyTSkT0g8DpOc3VxHw&sig=AOD64_2EsxrWR3pO78izPysWOwfskDObzg&q&adurl&ved=2ahUKEwjmq4D1hqOCAxVcJEQIHeVSCKkQ0Qx6BAgJEAE \"https://www.googleadservices.com/pagead/aclk?sa=L&ai=DChcSEwixqIf1hqOCAxWcAa0GHShoCYAYABACGgJwdg&gclid=CjwKCAjw7oeqBhBwEiwALyHLM1DNptS0iB-D6AtfkTWq-VQATKNUDrIC1e9c-uQZ9ihYnIFkkZRo1RoCzrMQAvD_BwE&ohost=www.google.com&cid=CAESV-D2E7mvwwka2aX5l98QaBPJPDPKssYk8phlIsqH5pC3RKvnIZAiXOT2GAhDC2jOE0-BlL1sXwLIpPoQxg6bOrmeIeH9ECbTc0jTNyTSkT0g8DpOc3VxHw&sig=AOD64_2EsxrWR3pO78izPysWOwfskDObzg&q&adurl&ved=2ahUKEwjmq4D1hqOCAxVcJEQIHeVSCKkQ0Qx6BAgJEAE\") to a degree that is unprecedented in Modern Mexican history.\n\nAs he made the transition from candidate to president, Lopez Obrador has gone from loudly advocating for a full investigation of the Ayotzinapa incident to actively defending the military’s reputation and outright denying that the government played a role in the disappearances.\n\n“In the case of Ayotzinapa and many other cases we can’t talk about crimes of the state,” [he explained](https://aristeguinoticias.com/2411/mexico/en-caso-ayotzinapa-ya-no-se-puede-hablar-de-crimen-de-estado-amlo/ \"https://aristeguinoticias.com/2411/mexico/en-caso-ayotzinapa-ya-no-se-puede-hablar-de-crimen-de-estado-amlo/\") shortly after taking office.\n\n[In a recent podcast interview](https://soundcloud.com/modern_mexico_podcast/episode-16-militarization-in-mexico \"https://soundcloud.com/modern_mexico_podcast/episode-16-militarization-in-mexico\"), [Stephanie Brewer](https://twitter.com/stephanie_wola \"https://twitter.com/stephanie_wola\"), a Mexico-focused security analyst at the [Washington Office on Latin America](https://www.wola.org/ \"https://www.wola.org/\"), and the author of a [recent report](https://www.wola.org/analysis/militarized-transformation-human-rights-and-democratic-controls-in-a-context-of-increasing-militarization-in-mexico/ \"https://www.wola.org/analysis/militarized-transformation-human-rights-and-democratic-controls-in-a-context-of-increasing-militarization-in-mexico/\") on the increasing power of the military in Mexico, explained that during Lopez Obrador’s time in office, “the military is growing in power without effective civilian controls. That is very worrying.”\n\nAsked to describe the military’s role in modern Mexico in three words, Brewer says, “I would think of the words empowerment, deference and denial. Lopez Obrador has ceded ever more civilian tasks to the military. Today, Mexico no longer has civilian police at the federal level. The military controls a range of tasks. At the same time, he is really following the lead of the military.”\n\nLopez Obrador’s presidency has logged over 165,000 murders. With over a year to go in his term, Lopez Obrador’s is already marked as the most violent sexenio in Modern Mexican history. It doesn’t seem like people around the world really grasp the severity of the violence Mexico is experiencing.\n\nPresident Lopez Obrador's administration has corresponded with the highest level of violence recorded in modern Mexican history.\n\nChart by Nathaniel Parish Flannery\n\nBrewer explains why Lopez Obrador’s embrace of the military has not corresponded with a significant improvement in the security dynamic in Mexico.\n\n“Militarization does not work and is not the solution. Why? The true power of organized crime doesn’t come just from firearms, it comes from collusion and corruption on the part of authority. It comes from a climate of near total impunity,” Brewer says.\n\nMexico sits at 115th place in the 2022 World Justice Project [rule of law index](https://worldjusticeproject.org/rule-of-law-index/country/2022/Mexico/ \"https://worldjusticeproject.org/rule-of-law-index/country/2022/Mexico/\"), worse than Russia and Sierra Leone.\n\n“We need to see a change in this model such that the emphasis is on strengthening rule of law through criminal investigations, through the criminal justice system, through civilian police, through intelligence to build cases to dismantle networks of organized crime,” she adds.\n\nLopez Obrador’s frantic scramble to send in soldiers to help restore order in Acapulco after the devastating damage from Hurricane Otis highlights the face that militarization in Mexico is a smokescreen to mask a deeper and longstanding problem of weak state capacity and weak institutions. Troop deployments can’t be a substitute for difficult institutional reforms.\n\n“Part of the problem of militarization is everything that is de-prioritized when you are investing all of your capital in the military. State and local level institutions haven’t had this same investment and support as militarization. I can’t emphasize enough the need for local and state institutions to be strengthened,” Brewer says.\n\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10500 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
