{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_142","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":5000,"start_index":4500,"url":"https://latamjournalismreview.org/articles/how-press-freedom-in-mexico-eroded-during-lopez-obradors-presidency/"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://latamjournalismreview.org/articles/how-press-freedom-in-mexico-eroded-during-lopez-obradors-presidency/:\nl). Dec. 9. Jaime Daniel Castaño Zacarías, in Zacatecas (digital)\n\n###### 2021 May 3. Benjamín Morales Hernández, in Sonora (digital) June 17. Gustavo Sánchez Cabrera, in Oaxaca (digital) June 22nd. Saúl Tijerina Rentería, in Coahuila (digital) July 22. Ricardo López Domínguez, in Sonora (digital) Aug. 19. Jacinto Romero Flores, in Veracruz (traditional radio) Oct. 28. Fredy López Arévalo, in Chiapas (print) Oct. 31. Alfredo Cardoso Echeverría, in Guerrero (digital)\n\n###### 2022 Jan. 10. José Luis Arenas Gamboa, in Veracruz (digital) Jan. 17. Margarito Martínez, in Baja California (independent) Jan. 23rd. Lourdes Maldonado Lopez, in Baja California (digital) Jan. 31st. Roberto Toledo, in Michoacán (digital) Feb. 10. Heber López Vázquez, in Oaxaca (digital) Feb. 24. Jorge Luis Camero Zazueta, in Sonora (digital) March 4. Juan Carlos Muñiz, in Zacatecas (digital) March 15. Armando Linares López, in Michoacán (digital) May 5. Luis Enrique Ramírez Ramos, in Sinaloa (print and digital) June 29. Antonio de la Cruz, in Tamaulipas (print) Aug. 11. Allan Gonzalez, in Chihuahua (traditional radio) Aug. 16. Juan Arjon Lopez, in Sonora (digital) Aug. 22. Fredid Román Román, in Guerrero (print)\n\n###### 2023 May 23rd. Marco Aurelio Ramirez Hernandez, in Puebla (independent) July 7. Luis Martin Sanchez Iñiguez, in Nayarit (print) July 15. Nelson Matus Peña, in Guerrero (digital) Sept. 25. Jesús Gutiérrez Vergara, in Sonora (digital) Nov. 16. Ismael Villagómez Tapia, in Chihuahua (digital)\n\n###### 2024 April 26th. Roberto Carlos Figueroa, in Morelos (digital) June 28th. Víctor Alfonso Culebro Morales, in Chiapas (digital and traditional radio) Aug. 4. Alejandro Alfredo Martínez Noguez, in Guanajuato (digital)\n\n### Missing journalists, another unresolved issue\n\n[Mexico also leads the list of missing members of the press](https://latamjournalismreview.org/articles/mexico-is-the-country-with-the-most-missing-journalists-in-the-world-cases-have-100-impunity/). By April this year, [Reporters Without Borders (RSF) recorded 31 journalists who had disappeared in Mexico since 1989](https://rsf.org/es/m%C3%A9xico-casi-seis-a%C3%B1os-de-amlo-en-el-poder-y-ning%C3%BAn-avance-en-la-protecci%C3%B3n-de-los-periodistas). This accounts for nearly [a third of journalists missing worldwide](https://rsf.org/es/balance-2023-45-periodistas-asesinados-en-el-ejercicio-de-sus-funciones-en-todo-el-mundo-una-cifra).\n\nDuring López Obrador's term, at least 10 media workers were reported missing, according to RSF’s tally. Of those, five are still missing: Jorge Molontzin Centlal, Pablo Felipe Romero Chávez, Roberto Carlos Flores Mendoza, Juan Carlos Hinojosa Viveros, and Alan García Aguilar.\n\n![Graphic of data about missing journalists in Mexico.](https://latamjournalismreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Articulo-19-Periodistas-desaparecidos.png)\n\nAccording to Article 19, at least four journalists are still missing by the end of the current administration. (Photo: Article 19, CC BY 2.5 MX, via Wikimedia Commons)\n\nMolontzin Centlal, a native of Chiapas, [disappeared on March 10, 2021](https://latamjournalismreview.org/es/news/periodista-mexicano-jorge-molontzin-desaparecido-en-sonora-desde-el-10-de-marzo/). He lived in the state of Sonora, where he worked as a reporter for Confidencial magazine in the city of Caborca.\n\nAlso in Sonora, [Romero Chávez disappeared on March 25, 2021](https://latamjournalismreview.org/news/police-reporter-is-missing-in-sonora-mexico/). After a three-year break from journalism, he had returned to work as a crime reporter at a radio station in the port city of Guaymas and for the newspaper El Vigía.\n\nFlores Mendoza was reported missing on Sept. 20, 2022, in the town of Comitán de Domínguez, Chiapas, after being intercepted while driving his car. Flores Mendoza ran the Facebook page Chiapas Denuncia Ya, where, days before his disappearance, [he had posted information about an alleged internal struggle within a criminal group](https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/desaparece-otro-comunicador-roberto-carlos-flores-mendoza/), according to press reports.\n\nThe most recent disappearance is that of Hinojosa Viveros, a photographer and editor of the digital outlet La de 8 News in Veracruz. Hinojosa Viveros was last seen on July 6, 2023, in the town of Nanchital, where he lived.\n\nArticle 19’s list also includes Alan García Aguilar, who was abducted by a criminal group on Dec. 25, 2022, alongside two other men identified as Fernando Moreno Villegas and Jesús Pintor Alegre, in the region known as Tierra Caliente in the state of Guerrero.\n\nOn Jan. 7, 2023, a video was posted on the Facebook page Escenario Calentano, which García Aguilar had founded in 2018 to publish content about organized crime. [The video showed García Aguilar and Moreno Villegas handcuffed and barefoot](https://x.com/LaGrillo90/status/1612997594057420801), saying they were “paying the consequences for their publications.”\n\nMoreno Villegas and Pintor Alegre were rel\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 9500 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
